  "sampradayas_title": "Sampradayas",
  "sampradayas_description": "According to Gita 4.2, Sampradayas (spiritual lineages) preserve and transmit sacred knowledge, ensuring its authenticity and continuity, thereby guiding seekers on the path to self-realization.",
  "sampradayas_1":"Origins and History",
  "sampradayas_2":"Beliefs and practices",
  "sampradayas_3":"Traditions",
  "shaiv_head":"Part of a series of Shaivism",
  "shaiv_1":"The origins of Shaivism are unclear and a matter of debate among scholars, as it is an amalgam of pre-Vedic cults and traditions and Vedic culture",
  "shaiv_2":"Shaivism, organized worship of the Indian god Shiva and, with Vaishnavism and Shaktism, one of the three principal forms of modern Hinduism. Shaivism includes such diverse movements as the highly philosophical Shaiva- siddhanta, the socially distinctive Lingayat, ascetics such as the dashnami sannyasins, and innumerable folk variants.",
  "shaiv_3":"Shaivism is ancient, and over time it developed many sub-traditions. These broadly existed and are studied in three groups: theistic dualism, nontheistic monism, and those that combine features or practices of the two.",
  "shakt_head":"Part of a series of Shaktism",
  "shakt_info":"Shaktism involves a galaxy of goddesses, all being regarded as different aspects, manifestations, or personifications of the divine feminine energy called Shakti. It includes various modes of worship, ranging from those focused on the most worshipped Durga, to gracious Parvati, and the fierce Kali. After the decline of Buddhism in India, various Hindu and Buddhist goddesses were combined to form the Mahavidya, a pantheon of ten goddesses. The most common forms of the Mahadevi worshipped in Shaktism include: Durga, Kali, Saraswati, Lakshmi, Parvati, and Tripurasundari. Also worshipped are the various Gramadevatas across the Indian villages.",
  "shakt_1":"The earliest archaeological evidence of what appears to be an Upper Paleolithic shrine for Shakti worship were discovered in the terminal upper paleolithic site of Baghor I (Baghor stone) in Sidhi district of Madhya Pradesh, India.",
  "shakt_2":"Shaktism believes in Shakti, the divine feminine power, as the ultimate reality and source of all creation. Its practices include goddess worship, tantric rituals, and celebrations like Durga Puja to honor manifestations of Shakti such as Durga, Kali, and Lakshmi.",
  "shakt_3":"Shaktism is a major Hindu tradition that reveres Shakti, the divine feminine energy, as the ultimate reality and cosmic power behind creation, sustenance, and destruction. It encompasses devotional worship, tantric practices, and festivals like Navaratri and Durga Puja, celebrating goddesses such as Durga, Kali, and Lalita Tripura Sundari as manifestations of supreme divinity.",
  "vaishnav_head":"Part of a series of Vaishnavism",
  "vaishnav_info":"One of the major Hindu denominations along with Shaivism, Shaktism, and Smartism. It is also called Vishnuism since it considers Vishnu as the sole supreme being leading all other Hindu deities, that is, Mahavishnu. Its followers are called Vaishnavites or Vaishnavas, and it includes sub-sects like Krishnaism and Ramaism, which consider Krishna and Rama as the supreme beings respectively. According to a 2020 estimate by The World Religion Database (WRD), hosted at Boston University's Institute on Culture, Religion and World Affairs (CURA), Vaishnavism is the largest Hindu sect, constituting about 399 million Hindus.",
  "vaishnav_1":"The ancient emergence of Vaishnavism is unclear, the evidence inconsistent and scanty. Syncretism of various traditions resulted in Vaishnavism. Although Vishnu was a Vedic solar deity, he is mentioned less often compared to Agni, Indra, and other Vedic deities, thereby suggesting that he had a minor position in the Vedic religion.",
  "vaishnav_2":"Vaishnavism is centred on the devotion of Vishnu and his avatars.\nAccording to Schweig, it is a \"polymorphic monotheism, i.e. a theology that recognises many forms (ananta rupa) of the one, single unitary divinity,\" since there are many forms of one original deity, with Vishnu taking many forms.",
  "vaishnav_3":"The Vaishnavism traditions may be grouped within four sampradayas, each exemplified by a specific Vedic personality. They have been associated with a specific founder, providing the following scheme: Sri Sampradaya (Ramanuja), Brahma Sampradaya (Madhvacharya), Rudra Sampradaya (Vishnuswami, Vallabhacharya), Kumaras Sampradaya (Nimbarka). These four sampradayas emerged in early centuries of the 2nd millennium CE, by the 14th century, influencing and sanctioning the Bhakti movement.",
  "smarth_head":"Part of a series of Smarthism",
  "smarth_info":"The Smarta tradition (Sanskrit: स्मार्त), also called Smartism, is a movement in Hinduism that developed and expanded with the Puranas genre of literature. It reflects a synthesis of four philosophical strands, namely Uttara Mīmāmsā, Advaita, Yoga, and theism. The Smarta tradition rejects theistic sectarianism, and is notable for the domestic worship of five shrines with five deities, all treated as equal - Ganesha, Shiva, Shakti, Vishnu and Surya. The Smarta tradition contrasted with the older Shrauta tradition, which was based on elaborate rituals and rites. There has been a considerable overlap in the ideas and practices of the Smarta tradition with other significant historic movements within Hinduism, namely Shaivism, Brahmanism, Vaishnavism, and Shaktism.",
  "smarth_1":"The smriti texts of the period between 200 BCE and 100 CE proclaim the authority of the Vedas, and nonrejection of the Vedas comes to be one of the most important touchstones for defining Hinduism over and against the heterodoxies, which rejected the Vedas. The Smriti texts interpret the Vedas in a number of ways, which gave rise to six darsanas (orthodox schools) of Hindu philosophy.",
  "smarth_2":"The Smartas evolved a kind of worship which is known as Panchayatana puja. In this Puja, one or more of the five Hindu Deities (Surya, Shiva, Vishnu, Ganesha and Adi Shakti) are the objects of veneration. The five symbols of the major Gods are placed on a round open metal dish called Panchayatana, the symbol of the deity preferred by the worshiper being in the center.",
  "smarth_3":"Smarta tradition emerged initially as a synthesis movement to unify Hinduism into a nonsectarian form based on the Vedic heritage. It accepted varnasrama-dharma, states Bruce Sullivan, which reflected an acceptance of Varna (caste/class) and ashrama (four stages of human life) as a form of social and religious duty. In the later second half of the 1st millennium, Adi Shankara reformed and brought ideas to the movement in the form of the Advaita Vedanta philosophy.",
  "gan_head":"Part of a series of Ganpatya",
  "gan_info_1":"When Lord Ganesha says in his Gita, the atrocities of the demons increase, and at that time, the Lord Himself takes the form of Sagun-Sakar incarnation, performing war-like pastimes. But in the present Kali Yuga, or in the history of the world, there are often no such direct demons on earth. The gods are in heaven, and the demons are in Patala, performing their duties peacefully.",
  "gan_info_2":"Therefore, the Lord does not need to take such an incarnation. However, even when the gods and demons are peaceful in their respective places, a third component, the human, behaves strangely. Some humans try to become god-like through their good deeds, while others behave more horribly than demons. At such times, there is no need to kill them physically. The task is to transform their distorted thoughts into good ones. Therefore, when the Lord takes a human incarnation at such times, it is called \"Shri Guru\".",
  "gan_info_title":"According to the Ganapatya tradition: ",
  "gan_info_title_1":"Pasha/Ganesh - incarnated as Shri Guru Ganakacharya",
  "gan_info_title_2":"Kamala/Brahma - incarnated as Shri Guru Bhrigu",
  "gan_info_title_3":"Ankush/Vishnu - incarnated as Shri Guru Vyas",
  "gan_info_title_4":"Mudgara/Surya - incarnated as Shri Guru Mudgalacharya",
  "gan_info_title_5":"Parasu/Shiva - incarnated as Shri Guru Shukacharya",
  "gan_info_title_1_":"The incarnations of Guru Panchaka in Kali Yuga:",
  "gan_info_title_1_1":"Shri Guru Ganakacharya - incarnated as Gaudapadacharya",
  "gan_info_title_1_2":"Shri Guru Mudgalacharya - incarnated as Ganeshyogindra",
  "gan_info_title_1_3":"Shri Guru Shukacharya - incarnated as Shankaracharya",
  "gan_info_title_1_4":"Shri Guru Vyas - incarnated as Girijasuta",
  "gan_info_title_1_5":"Shri Guru Bhrigu - incarnated as Brahmananda",
  "gan_1":"The origins of Ganpatya are unclear and a matter of debate among scholars, as it is an amalgam of pre-Vedic cults and traditions and Vedic culture",
  "gan_2":"Ganpatya includes organized worship of Ganesha and deep philosophical traditions, rooted in Hinduism.",
  "gan_3":"Ganpatya is ancient and evolved into multiple traditions over time, blending dualism and monism practices. Currently, Mayureshwar Parampara from Morgao,Pune is spreading knowledge and devtion of Ganesha.",
