  "sattva_title":"Sattva \n(Purity and Harmony)",
  "sattva_desc":"Sattva is the guna of purity, harmony, \nand balance. It fosters clarity, wisdom, \nand selflessness, leading to \nspiritual growth and inner peace.",
  "shlok_s":"\"Sattvam sukhe sanjayati\" \n(Bhagavad Gita 14.9)",
  "shlok_sm":"Sattva binds one to joy and knowledge. It promotes a state of harmony, mental clarity, and spiritual awareness.",
  "sattva_sym":"- Clarity of thought and decision-making.\n- Desire for spiritual growth and knowledge.\n- Emotional stability, with an ability to handle challenges calmly.\n- Seeing situations with a clear, unbiased perspective.\n- A sense of satisfaction and fulfillment with life.\n- No attachment to personal recognition or fame.\n- Feeling a deeper connection to spirituality or the divine.\n- A natural feeling of gratitude towards life and others.\n- Thoughtful actions that promote peace and harmony in others.\n",
  "sattva_how":"How to Cultivate Sattva ?",
  "sattva_how_":"1. Practice meditation and mindfulness regularly.\n2. Eat fresh, light, and nutritious food.\n3. Engage in acts of selflessness and kindness.\n4. Maintain cleanliness and live in harmony with nature.",
  "rajas_title":"Rajas \n(Activity and Passion)",
  "rajas_desc":"Rajas represents energy, passion, and activity. It drives ambition, effort, and desires, but can lead to restlessness and attachment to outcomes.",
  "shlok_r":"\"Rajah karmani bharata\" \n(Bhagavad Gita 14.9)",
  "shlok_rm":"Rajas binds one to activity and desires. It fuels ambition and dynamism but can result in agitation and dissatisfaction due to unfulfilled cravings.",
  "rajas_sym":"- Desire for material success and recognition.\n- Anxiety and dissatisfaction when goals are unmet.\n- Inability to focus or calm the mind, constantly jumping between thoughts.\n- A constant drive to achieve more, often leading to burnout.\n- Getting easily frustrated or upset by setbacks.\n- An overwhelming urge to speak without pause, often due to anxiety or excitement.\n- An unending desire for external validation.\n- Making decisions quickly without considering the long-term consequences.\n- Over-prioritizing material success and wealth.\n",
  "rajas_how":"How to Balance Rajas ?",
  "rajas_how_":"1. Practice yoga and pranayama to calm the mind.\n2. Avoid overstimulation and excessive desires.\n3. Focus on detaching from the results of actions.\n4. Meditate regularly to cultivate inner peace.",
  "tamas_title":"Tamas \n(Inertia and Ignorance)",
  "tamas_desc":"Tamas is the guna of darkness, inertia, and ignorance. It brings lethargy, confusion, and resistance to change, leading to stagnation and delusion.",
  "shlok_t":"\"Tamah pramade sanjayatyuta\" \n(Bhagavad Gita 14.9)",
  "shlok_tm":"Tamas binds one to ignorance and delusion. It clouds judgment, causing lethargy, confusion, and indulgence in destructive habits.",
  "tamas_sym":"- Attraction to destructive or harmful behaviors.\n- Attraction to destructive or harmful behaviors.\n- Lack of motivation and apathy towards personal goals.\n- Difficulty focusing or comprehending simple tasks (mental fog).\n- Avoidance of responsibility or decision-making.\n- Tendency to dwell on negative thoughts without clear reason.\n- Extreme fear or resistance to change or new experiences.\n- Seeking distractions or addictions to avoid facing reality.\n- Feelings of depression or hopelessness about the future.\n",
  "tamas_how":"How to Overcome Tamas ?",
  "tamas_how_":"1. Engage in regular physical activity to break inertia.\n2. Consume fresh and healthy food; avoid processed or stale food.\n3. Practice meditation to develop mental clarity.\n4. Surround yourself with positive and inspiring individuals.",
