  "yoga_quiz_title" :"Yoga Path Quiz",
  "yoga_quiz_desc" : "\nThis quiz offers an engaging and interactive way for users to explore their recommended path of Yoga based on their preferences and values.\n\nBy answering a series of houghtfully crafted questions, users receive a personalized result highlighting the Yoga path\nJnana, Karma, or Bhakti\nthat resonates most with their choices.\n",
  "garbha_desc":"The term Garbhsanskar is often heard these days, but most people only have a superficial understanding of it.\nLiterally, it means the sanskars (values or influences) imparted to the fetus in the womb. However, what kind of sanskars can be imparted to the fetus, and how? What is their significance, and why are they necessary? It's essential to understand these aspects.",
  "ritual":"Ritualistic Perspective",
  "ritual_1_title":"\"Pumān sūyate yasmāt iti puṁsavanam\"",
  "ritual_1_trans":"The process through which a \"pumān\" (a valiant and \nvirtuous progeny) is born is called Puṁsavana.",
  "rirual_1_desc":"In practice and Ayurveda, this samskāra is described as a ritual for achieving the desired gender of the child. It is performed to determine whether the unborn child should be male or female. However, the Vedas suggest that this samskāra should be performed for everyone with the intention of obtaining a virtuous and radiant child. \nHence, it is advised that this ritual be performed in the second, fourth, sixth, or eighth month of pregnancy.\n\nAccording to Ayurveda, the gender of the fetus is determined two months after conception. This means that within one or one and a half months after the cessation of menstruation, it can be inferred whether the fetus will be male or female. It is believed that prior to this stage, the gender of the fetus can be influenced or altered. However, if the ritual were solely for altering the gender, there would be no recommendation to perform Puṁsavana in the fourth, sixth, or eighth month.",
  "ritual_2_title":"\"Avyaktaḥ prathame māsi saptāhāt kalalī bhavet।\n Garbhaḥ puṁsavānānyaya pūrva vyakte prayojayet॥\"",
  "ritual_2_trans":"In the first month, the fetus remains undifferentiated in terms of gender. In the second month, the differentiation starts becoming apparent. Hence, the Puṁsavana samskāra should ideally be performed before the gender of the fetus becomes evident",
  "ritual_2_desc":"The ancient sciences revealed that the gender of the fetus could be influenced, making this knowledge accessible to all.In today's society, the ratio of males to females has become imbalanced. The number of females is decreasing rapidly, creating a serious concern for the future.In ancient times, the number of females was greater than males. Boys were given more importance, partly because males frequently lost their lives in wars or during long journeys. However, in the current scenario, there is no necessity to perform Puṁsavana purely for ensuring a male child.That said, Puṁsavana is not limited to altering gender; it has greater benefits, such as ensuring the radiance, virtues, and well-being of the fetus. It also helps prevent genetic defects (genetic disorders) and protects the fetus.",
  "ritual_3_title":"Mamā bhāryāyām utpattyasya - mānsya garbhasya vāyika - gārbhika doṣa - parihārārthaṃ puṃ rūpatā jñānodaya pratirodha parihāra dvārā Śrīparameśvara prītyarthaṃ puṃsavanākhyam karma āhaṃ kariṣye. Tadaṅgatvena Gaṇapati pūjanam, svasti vācanam, Mātṛkā pūjanam, Nāndīśrāddham ca kariṣye.",
  "ritual_3_trans":"For the sake of my wife's pregnancy, to prevent mental and physical defects in the fetus, and to promote the birth of a male child with good intellect and character, I shall perform the Pumsavana ritual to please Lord Parameshwara. As part of this ritual, I shall perform the worship of Lord Ganesha, recite auspicious verses, worship the goddess Matruka, and offer oblations to the ancestors.",
  "ritual_3_desc":"Sankalp means – express the purpose for which this sanskar is being performed. The statement of the aim while performing this sanskar is called Sankalp Karna. Sankalp is done while performing any sanskar, work or ritual.",
  "philosophy":"Philosphy",
  "philosophy_desc":"Listening to Atmagyan (self-knowledge) during \npregnancy is crucial as it calms the mind, reduces \nstress and anxiety, and promotes emotional well-being.\n\nThis positivity is transmitted to the fetus, \nshaping its personality and intellect. It also \nprepares the expectant mother for \na mindful and joyful parenthood experience.\n\nThis soothing audio excerpt from Swami Sarvapriyananda's enlightening lecture on 'Our Real Nature', where he explains the profound concept of Atmagyan in a simple and accessible manner, delivered in his calming and serene voice.",
  "devotional":"During pregnancy, devotion plays a vital role in nurturing a positive and calm environment. It helps the expectant mother connect with the divine, reducing stress and anxiety. A devoted mindset fosters emotional well-being, benefiting both the mother and the unborn child's spiritual growth.\n\nNurture Your Spirit: A Guiding Resource \nfor Devotion During Pregnancy.\n\nDownload this insightful PDF guide, specially designed to support expectant mothers on their spiritual journey. Discover practical tips and inspiration to cultivate devotion, inner peace, and a deeper connection with yourself and your unborn child.\n",
  "download": "Download",
  "ayurvedic": "Ayurvedic",
  "ayurvedic_desc": "During pregnancy, following an Ayurvedic diet is crucial for a healthy mother and baby. It helps maintain digestive balance, boosts immunity, and ensures optimal nutrient absorption. \n\nA customized Ayurvedic diet also alleviates pregnancy-related discomforts, promoting a smooth and joyful gestation period. \nDownload Your Free PDF Guide: Ayurvedic Nutrition & Precautions for a Healthy Pregnancy, Month by Month\n",
